WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.100
Noah Baumbach's "Marriage Story" is less
about marriage than it is about divorce,

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.380
depicting the souring of what started as
an amicable split between an actress and

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.400
her theater-directing husband. Like 69%
of divorces in the United States, the wife

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.100
initiated the separation. We learned
that she felt oppressed in her marriage,

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.660
believing that her career and ambitions
were consumed by those of her husband.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.300
But the marriage was fairly egalitarian
by modern sensibilities, with both

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.710
husband and wife working and sharing
domestic responsibilities. This gets at

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.370
what is one of the most important social
issues shown in the film, though likely

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.540
not Baumbach's intention. Studies show
that women on the whole seem to have

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.010
lower satisfaction rates with their
marriages than men. Even after decades of

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.850
men doing more housework and more
childcare, and women spending more time

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.540
on their careers,
many women seem no happier than

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.220
a half-century ago, and maybe even less so.
Maybe it's that society has created

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.820
unrealistic expectations for women.
Marriage not only means finding

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.460
a soul mate and a helpful father and a breadwinner,

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.740
it is also an exercise in self-fulfillment.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.720
Did you think the movie "Marriage Story" was
an accurate depiction of marriage today?

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.920
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.460
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for
